# Real-World Analogies for Technical Concepts

When introducing any computer science or software engineering concept, explain it using an intuitive real-world analogy before discussing technical code.

- **Databases**: Index = Textbook index at the back; Transactions = Bank wire transfers; Caching = Desk-top inbox tray vs. basement archives.
- **Networking**: Client-Server = Restaurant Diner & Kitchen; APIs = Waiter & Menu; WebSockets = Live phone call; TCP = Certified postal mail with signature.
- **Concurrency**: Async/Promises = Restaurant buzzer pager; Race conditions = Two people grabbing the last soda simultaneously; Mutex = Restroom door latch.
- **Security**: JWT = Tamper-proof club wristband; SQLi = Malicious text on a name tag; Asymmetric encryption = Mailbox slot (anyone drops in, only owner has key).

Format as:
```text
✦ Concept & Analogy: <Topic>
Analogy: [Everyday physical comparison]
Why this matters: [Practical implication in this project]
```
